/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Blood carries three formed elements.
   - Manual counting uses a haemocytometer: a glass slide with a ruled Neubauer grid and a chamber 0.1 mm deep.
   - N is the number of cells counted; D is the dilution factor; V is the counted volume in µL of diluted sample; 106 converts cells per µL to cells per litre.
   - A Coulter counter pulls diluted blood through a single aperture, about 100 µm across, in a glass tube.
   - The pulse is amplified and fed to threshold discriminators.
   - ΔV is the pulse height in volts; I is the constant current in amperes; ΔR is the rise in resistance in ohms when a cell displaces electrolyte.
   - Two cells passing together make one pulse, not two.
   - A modern analyser combines impedance with flow cytometry.
   - A three-part differential groups white cells into lymphocytes, monocytes and granulocytes.
   - Semi-automatic counters need the operator to dilute the sample and press a button.
*/
