/* book exam traps: Forgetting the dilution factor D. Multiply by it once; the diluted count is not the blood count. | Using the blood volume instead of the diluted volume in the formula. V is the volume actually counted in the chamber or aperture. | Reporting per millilitre when the report asks per litre. Multiply per mL by 1000. | Trusting a count from a partly clogged aperture. Coincidence biases counts low; debris in a partial clog adds noise pulses and changes pulse heights, so the analyser watches the aperture current and the time taken to meter the volume. | Setting thresholds by eye. Platelets and small red cells overlap; set thresholds with calibration particles. */
